<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kū</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kūkāʻilimoku</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kū is one of the four great gods of Hawaiian religion, with Kanaloa, Kāne and Lono, the god of war, politics, farming and fishing. He was worshipped under many names that refer to his manifestations in forest and rain, husbandry, fishing, war and sorcery, among them Kūkāʻilimoku, the &quot;Snatcher of Land&quot;, whose rituals included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a>, which was not part of the worship of other gods. Kūkāʻilimoku was the guardian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a>, who unified the Hawaiian archipelago under one ruler and raised monuments to him in Kona. Three colossal statues of Kū dedicated by Kamehameha were reunited at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a> in 2010.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ku-hawaiian-god.jpg" alt="Kū" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lebrac · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kuka'ilimoku.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the island of Hawaiʻi whom his uncle, the ruling chief Kalaniʻōpuʻu, had made guardian of the war god <b>Kūkāʻilimoku</b>, fell out with Kalaniʻōpuʻu’s heir after the old ruler’s death in 1782 and defeated him, his cousin Kīwalaʻō, at the Battle of Mokuʻōhai, winning the west and north of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hawaii">Kingdom of Hawaii · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1782 Kamehameha, guardian of the war god <b>Kūkāʻilimoku</b>, defeated his cousin Kīwalaʻō, Kalaniʻōpuʻu’s son and successor, at Mokuʻōhai, and by 1795, with Western guns, ships and advisers, he had conquered every main island but Kauaʻi and Niʻihau, whose ruler accepted him as overlord in 1810.</blockquote><a href="/societies/hawaii">Social Hierarchy of Hawaii under the Kapu</a></li></ul></section>
